---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import CategoryHub from '../../components/CategoryHub.astro';
---
<Base
  title="FRP Sheet Resin Manufacturer in India | Sheet Grade Resins"
  description="Samrat Poly Resins manufactures sheet-grade polyester resins in India for FRP roofing, cladding, translucent roof-light panels and coloured sheet."
>
  <CategoryHub
    category="Sheet Grade Resins"
    slug="sheet-grade-resins"
    heading="Sheet Grade Resin — FRP Roofing, Cladding & Panels"
    intro="Sheet grade describes a resin matched to continuous lamination and panel work rather than general moulding: reactivity tuned to line speed, wet-out fast enough for chopped strand mat as it passes, and colour or clarity controlled at the factory instead of on the shop floor. Samrat Poly Resins publishes three sheet grades at Doraha, Punjab, and the choice between them is decided by what the finished sheet has to do about light and sunlight."
    note="Sheet grade is a market term rather than a standardised designation. For the finished product, IS 12866 is the Indian standard covering translucent glass-reinforced polyester sheet, and it sets glass content, light transmission class and ageing criteria that belong to the tested sheet, not to a drum of resin."
    gridHeading="Published sheet grades"
    chooseHeading="Which sheet grade does your panel need?"
    collectionDescription="Samrat Poly Resins' published sheet-grade unsaturated polyester resins for FRP roofing, cladding, translucent roof-light panels and coloured sheet manufacturing."
    enquiryContext="sheet-grade-family"
    enquiryPlaceholder="Finished sheet or panel, process and line speed, reinforcement, exposure, colour standard, quantity, delivery location"
    resourceSlugs={[
      'what-is-sheet-grade-yellow-resin',
      'what-is-uv-stabilized-sheet-grade-resin',
      'uv-stabilized-resin-vs-uv-curing-resin',
      'uv-stabilized-vs-standard-sheet-grade-yellow-resin',
      'how-uv-degrades-frp-sheets',
      'what-is-roof-light-sheet-grade-resin',
      'how-to-choose-resin-for-frp-sheet-and-panel-manufacturing',
    ]}
  >
    <Fragment slot="choose">
      <div class="hub-opt">
        <p class="hub-opt-name">Light through the sheet</p>
        <h3>Roof Light Sheet Grade Resin</h3>
        <p>
          A water-white grade for translucent roof-light panels, skylight sheets and
          greenhouse roofing, where the product is sold on how much daylight it passes.
        </p>
        <a href="/products/roof-light-resin/">View Roof Light Sheet Grade Resin</a>
      </div>
      <div class="hub-opt">
        <p class="hub-opt-name">Coloured sheet, general</p>
        <h3>Sheet Grade Yellow Resin</h3>
        <p>
          Pre-pigmented yellow, with the colour dispersed at the factory rather than added
          at the laminating stage. The general sheet and panel route where the finished
          part is not facing years of direct sunlight.
        </p>
        <a href="/products/sheet-grade-yellow-resin/">View Sheet Grade Yellow Resin</a>
      </div>
      <div class="hub-opt">
        <p class="hub-opt-name">Coloured sheet, outdoors</p>
        <h3>UV Stabilized Sheet Grade Yellow Resin</h3>
        <p>
          The same pre-pigmented yellow route with a light-stabiliser package, for roofing,
          cladding and coloured sheet that lives outdoors. It is UV <em>stabilised</em>, not
          UV curing: it cures conventionally with MEKP and cobalt, and no lamp is involved.
        </p>
        <a href="/products/uv-stabilized-sheet-grade-yellow-resin/">View UV Stabilized Sheet Grade Yellow Resin</a>
      </div>
      <p class="hub-caution">
        Two things no resin supplier can honestly promise for a sheet. Nobody can give a
        service life in years without a defined exposure site, laminate construction and a
        stated failure criterion, and accelerated weathering hours do not convert into
        calendar years — acceleration factors have to be measured, not calculated. Bulk
        stabilisation also does not replace a surfacing veil or a protective film: they
        defend against different mechanisms, and the film does not protect cut edges or
        fastener holes.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
  </CategoryHub>
</Base>
